 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Possessives take an article",
     "texto": [
      "In Italian, the possessive comes with an article, unlike English: **il mio libro** (my book), **la mia casa** (my house), **i miei amici** (my friends).",
      "The forms: **mio, mia** (my), **tuo, tua** (your), **suo, sua** (his/her). In the plural: **miei, tuoi, suoi** and **mie, tue, sue**.",
      "They agree with what is owned, not with the owner: **sua sorella** can mean «his sister» or «her sister»."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The family exception",
     "texto": [
      "As in English, with most family words in the singular the article is left out: **mio padre**, **mia madre**, **mio fratello**, **mia sorella**. The rule has exceptions: **loro** takes an article (**la loro madre**), and a diminutive or modifier usually takes one (**la mia sorellina**, **il mio caro fratello**).",
      "In the plural, the article is used: **i miei genitori** (my parents), **le mie sorelle** (my sisters)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
